<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">recipes</ansi>

Nobody hands you a cookbook. You learn a dish by trying a mix of
ingredients, or by reading a recipe page someone wrote down. Everything you
have learned goes in your recipe book, which a hearth and your campfire
both use.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Trying a new mix</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">cook [ingredient] [ingredient] ...</ansi>
  At a hearth, or at your own camp with its fire lit, cooks exactly the
  ingredients you name from your pack and company cargo. Name them by their
  short names (<ansi fg="command">cook meat thyme</ansi>) or with a count (<ansi fg="command">cook 2 meat thyme</ansi>).
  A pot takes at most 4 ingredients.

  If the mix is a dish you can make, you cook it, and the first time you
  do, you learn it: the recipe goes in your book and you will not need
  to name its ingredients again. If the mix matches nothing, you get a
  <ansi fg="itemname">makeshift meal</ansi>, plain food worth 25 Hunger that gives no buff.
  The ingredients are spent either way.

  A new dish above your Cooking level does not come out: the mix is only
  a makeshift meal, and you learn nothing. A dish you already know but
  lack the Cooking for is refused, and nothing is spent. At camp the
  best cook with you cooks, as with <ansi fg="command">camp cook</ansi>; at a hearth it is your
  own Cooking.

  Only raw game, fish and herbs go in the pot, and every mix needs some
  game or fish: herbs alone only season, so they are refused before
  anything is spent. A cooked meal, a drink or a tool does not go in.
  Filler words are fine: <ansi fg="command">cook meat and thyme</ansi>.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Cooking what you know</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">camp cook</ansi>
  <ansi fg="command">use hearth</ansi>
  Cook a dish already in your book, without naming ingredients. Dishes
  that need no more than Cooking 1 (seared game meat, grilled fish) are
  common knowledge. Every other dish must be learned first, even if you
  have the skill and the ingredients: a dish you have not learned is
  not cooked by a bare <ansi fg="command">camp cook</ansi>, by <ansi fg="command">use hearth</ansi>, or by a member on the cook duty.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Remedies are in the book too</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">camp prepare remedy with [herb] [herb] ...</ansi>
  Tries exactly that mix of herbs on the ailing members of your company
  (<ansi fg="command">help ailments</ansi>). If it is the remedy for an ailment someone has, it
  is one dose: it cures the first member it helps, uses exactly the herbs
  you named, and you learn the remedy; after that a plain
  <ansi fg="command">camp prepare remedy</ansi> makes it. Any other mix uses up its herbs and
  teaches nothing. Thyme tea, for the chill, is common knowledge.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Your recipe book</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">recipes</ansi>
  Lists every dish and remedy you have learned and what it takes. The web client's
  Company window shows the same list on its Camp tab. <ansi fg="command">look hearth</ansi> lists
  only the dishes you know.

  Characters made before recipe discovery arrived keep every dish and
  remedy they could already make.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Recipe pages</ansi>

A recipe page teaches one dish when you <ansi fg="command">use</ansi> it, and crumbles as it does.
Merchants never buy a page back, nor a cooked meal. You still need the
Cooking level to make the dish.

Instrument pages work the same way: a fine instrument's page teaches its
recipe, and <ansi fg="command">music craft</ansi> makes it at camp (<ansi fg="command">help instruments</ansi>).

See also: <ansi fg="command">help instruments</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help cooking</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help camp</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help camp duties</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help use</ansi>,
<ansi fg="command">help skills</ansi>.
